<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ptolemy II Philadelphus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">309–<a href="/years/246-bce">246 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ptolemy II Philadelphus was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from 283 to 246 BCE, born on Kos. He expanded the Library and Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and extended Ptolemaic power through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, where the Nesiotic League honoured him, and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, where he refounded Marion as Arsinoe. He eventually made peace with his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magas-of-cyrene">Magas of Cyrene</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus.jpg" alt="Ptolemy II Philadelphus" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ptolemy_II_MAN_Napoli_Inv5600.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ptolemy II was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a>, the Macedonian general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a>, and of his third wife, Berenice I. He was born on Kos in 309 or 308 BCE, during his father's invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> in the Fourth Diadoch War, and had two full sisters, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsinoe-ii">Arsinoe II</a> and Philotera. He was educated by distinguished intellectuals, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philitas-of-cos">Philitas of Cos</a> and Strato of Lampsacus. His older half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-keraunos">Ptolemy Keraunos</a> was heir presumptive at his birth, and a struggle for the succession ended with Keraunos leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> around 287 BCE. Ptolemy I had Ptolemy II declared king and co-regent on 28 March 284 BCE, and the co-regency lasted until his father's death in 282 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He pursued an aggressive foreign policy with mixed success. Around 275 BCE Ptolemaic forces invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> and annexed the northern twelve miles of the Triakontaschoinos, the stretch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> between the First and Second Cataract, whose gold mines helped the prosperity of the kingdom. In the First Syrian War, from 274 to 271 BCE, he invaded Seleucid Syria, was defeated by Antiochus I and retreated, but Antiochus abandoned the war and agreed to peace. About 270 BCE he cleared the old canal from the Nile to the Gulf of Suez, and two missions went down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> coasts and set up a chain of harbour bases.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chremonidean-war">Chremonidean War</a>, about 267 to 261 BCE, he confronted Antigonid Macedonia for control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and suffered serious setbacks. The Second Syrian War, from 260 to 253 BCE, lost many of the gains of the first, including sections of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamphylia">Pamphylia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>. He died on 28 January 246 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Ptolemy II transformed the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> into a state cult of the dynasty. He deified his father and, after her death, his mother Berenice I, and about 272 BCE made himself and his sister-wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsinoe-ii">Arsinoe II</a> divine as the Theoi Adelphoi. He also presented himself as a traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> and financed temple building across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, including the core of the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a>. He was an eager patron of scholarship, funding the expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria">Library of Alexandria</a>. The poets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callimachus">Callimachus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theocritus">Theocritus</a>, Apollonius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and Posidippus received stipends, and the mathematician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclid">Euclid</a> worked under his patronage. The tradition that he prompted the Greek translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> is unlikely to be true.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ptolemy II Philadelphus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ashoka's thirteenth rock edict names the Hellenistic kings Antiochus II, <b>Ptolemy II</b>, Antigonus Gonatas, Magas of Cyrene and Alexander of Epirus as recipients of his dhamma missions, which shows the empire's dealings with the wider world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The early Ptolemies strengthened their control through military power and diplomatic alliances, and by the reign of <b>Ptolemy II Philadelphus</b> (r. 283–246 BCE) they also ruled Cyrenaica, Cyprus, southern Syria and parts of the Aegean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Later he made peace with his half-brother <b>Ptolemy II</b> and betrothed his daughter Berenice to the heir, the future Ptolemy III.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cyrene-under-magas">Kingdom of Cyrene under Magas</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ptolemy II</b> refounded the site as Arsinoe c. 270 BCE, and the modern town of Polis stands there today.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-marion">Kingdom of Marion</a></li><li><blockquote>Kos was a favoured base of the Ptolemaic Empire, where <b>Ptolemy II</b> was born and the poet Philitas taught.</blockquote><a href="/state/kos">Kos</a></li><li><blockquote>After the collapse of Demetrius, the league passed under Ptolemaic control in the 280s BCE; officials called nesiarchs and Philocles, king of Sidon, acted for <b>Ptolemy II</b>, and the islanders founded the Ptolemaia festival.</blockquote><a href="/state/nesiotic-league">Nesiotic League</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kos. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=-269" aria-label="Alexandria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Ptolemy II Philadelphus"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4992 4898h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4992 4898h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=-269">What was here in 269 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
